WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.851
RTIC firmware often processes input from
UART, SPI, I2C, CAN, USB, BLE,

2
00:00:03.931 --> 00:00:07.841
radio packets, sensors, local controls,
maintenance commands, update channels,

3
00:00:07.921 --> 00:00:08.927
or network services.

4
00:00:09.007 --> 00:00:10.906
Some inputs arrive through interrupts.

5
00:00:10.986 --> 00:00:13.946
Others arrive through scheduled tasks or
peripheral drivers.

6
00:00:14.026 --> 00:00:16.540
All should be treated as unverified until
validated.

7
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.692
A message can be syntactically well formed
and still be too large,

8
00:00:19.772 --> 00:00:23.626
out of sequence, inconsistent with other
fields, not allowed in the current mode,

9
00:00:23.706 --> 00:00:25.941
or inappropriate for the current device
state.

10
00:00:26.021 --> 00:00:27.417
Parsing confirms structure.

11
00:00:27.497 --> 00:00:30.513
Validation confirms meaning, permission,
and timing context.

12
00:00:30.593 --> 00:00:34.224
Defensive RTIC designs separate fast
capture from heavier validation work.

13
00:00:34.304 --> 00:00:38.214
Interrupt-facing code should do the
minimum necessary to preserve data and signal

14
00:00:38.294 --> 00:00:38.573
work.

15
00:00:38.653 --> 00:00:40.888
More expensive parsing, validation,
logging,

16
00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:44.878
and state transitions should be bounded
and scheduled in a way that does not disrupt

17
00:00:44.958 --> 00:00:46.187
time-critical behavior.

18
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:48.724
State machines help make protocol flow
reviewable.

19
00:00:48.804 --> 00:00:52.603
They show which commands are valid in
which modes, what transitions are allowed,

20
00:00:52.683 --> 00:00:54.302
and how invalid input is rejected.

21
00:00:54.382 --> 00:00:58.292
Domain types can represent validated
messages so downstream tasks do not continue

22
00:00:58.372 --> 00:01:00.718
passing raw buffers and primitive values
around.

23
00:01:00.798 --> 00:01:03.200
Firmware paths should avoid panic-prone
parsing.

24
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.576
Invalid input should produce clear
rejection, controlled recovery,

25
00:01:06.656 --> 00:01:07.941
or safe degraded behavior.

26
00:01:08.021 --> 00:01:09.752
The goal is not to accept every input.

27
00:01:09.832 --> 00:01:13.072
The goal is to reject bad input without
making timing, availability,

28
00:01:13.152 --> 00:01:14.660
or device state unpredictable.
